  "findings": [
    "Both stock origins—delayed RAM work and ram_update_store—route through haptic_lifetime_request_ram_firmware. The candidate increments its callback counter before request_firmware_nowait and rolls it back on shutdown or synchronous submission error.",
    "The pinned target schedules the callback only after request setup succeeds. On success it invokes the callback, then drops the device reference and module reference. The candidate passes THIS_MODULE.",
    "The completion callback serializes with other RAM callbacks. After shutdown it releases firmware and skips ram_load; while active it calls ram_load. In either case it frees the request and decrements/wakes the callback counter after callback work ends.",
    "The target release_firmware implementation checks fw before dereferencing or freeing it, so the shutdown callback path safely accepts the NULL firmware value used for failed requests.",
    "Current aw_remove cancels delayed RAM work, waits for all tracked callbacks, cancels delayed work again, and only then destroys the private workqueue and retires device state."
  ],
  "conclusion": "The reviewed asynchronous callback/state lifetime path remains statically addressed in candidate 37fc86926a32dc3bd170d3c673a9adef3bdb4b48ff90eff1bff2a80af59caeca. Runtime callback scheduling, allocation-fault injection, unbind races, and hardware behavior remain untested. This report does not close unrelated probe/driver risks.",
